<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suez Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1869</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Suez Canal is a sea-level waterway across the Isthmus of Suez in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. Built by a French-led company under a concession granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-pasha">Said Pasha</a> in 1854, it opened in 1869 and transformed the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Asia, raising the value of ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a> nationalised it in 1956, and it was closed from 1967 to 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suez-canal.jpg" alt="Suez Canal" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iss016e019375.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
